@database "llm_openai"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_openai.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "OpenAI SDK provider"
@{h1}OpenAI SDK provider

This plugin lets agents talk to OpenAI through the official @{tt}openai@{utt}
Python SDK: Chat Completions for ordinary models, and the Realtime API for
the realtime models, which do not answer on Chat Completions. It is an LLM
provider plugin: it has no tools, no hooks and no panel. You use it by
writing @{tt}provider: openai@{utt} in a model entry of
@{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt}; agents reach that entry through a profile.

@{table}
Provider name | Kind | Endpoint
openai | chat | POST <base_url>/chat/completions through the SDK
openai | chat, realtime models | one WebSocket to <base_url>/realtime per call
openai | batch | delegated to llm_openai_compat's Batch API backend
@{body}

How models, profiles and fallback chains work in general is in
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}; this manual covers what
this provider does with an entry. For OpenRouter, DeepSeek, local servers,
reasoning replay or prompt-cache keys, the httpx routes of
@{" llm_openai_compat " link llm_openai_compat/main} are the better home:
this one has none of those.

@{code}
For users (the operator writing llm.yaml)
  @{"  Model entry keys  " link keys}
  @{"  API keys and endpoints  " link apikeys}
  @{"  Timeouts and streaming  " link timeouts}
  @{"  Retries  " link retries}
  @{"  The Realtime API  " link realtime}
  @{"  Usage, cost and the hooks  " link usage}
  @{"  Errors  " link errors}
  @{"  Batch  " link batch}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node keys "Model entry keys"
@{h1}Model entry keys

The keys of a model entry this provider reads. Everything else in
@{tt}LLMModelConfig@{utt} is either handled by the core (@{tt}extends@{utt},
profiles) or not read here.

@{table}
Key | Default | What the client does with it
provider | -- | openai
model | -- | the model id sent to the endpoint
api_key | from the endpoint's variable | see @{" API keys and endpoints " link apikeys}
base_url | https://api.openai.com/v1 | the endpoint of the SDK and of the Realtime socket
max_tokens | none | sent as @{tt}max_tokens@{utt} (Realtime: @{tt}max_output_tokens@{utt}); 0 or unset sends nothing
temperature | none | sent as given on Chat Completions; not sent on Realtime
modalities | none | sent as given on Chat Completions; not sent on Realtime
request_timeout | 120 | seconds, see @{" Timeouts and streaming " link timeouts}
context_window | the model-config default | only reported to the core, never sent
capabilities | all defaults | streaming decides the path; default_api_type picks Realtime; image_input and audio_input shape tool attachments; structured_output allows response formats; developer_role
@{body}

Keys that belong to other routes are logged at build time as "not wired ...
will be ignored" and then have no effect: @{tt}thinking_level@{utt},
@{tt}thinking_budget@{utt}, @{tt}include_thoughts@{utt},
@{tt}prompt_cache_key@{utt}, @{tt}prompt_cache_mode@{utt},
@{tt}tool_schema_dialect@{utt}, @{tt}assistant_reasoning_field@{utt},
@{tt}reasoning_details_mode@{utt}, @{tt}thinking_request_shape@{utt},
@{tt}stream_silence_timeout@{utt}, @{tt}provider_affinity_minutes@{utt}.
Not read at all, and not logged: @{tt}parallel_tool_calls@{utt},
@{tt}service_tier@{utt}, @{tt}safety_settings@{utt},
@{tt}safety_identifier@{utt}, @{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt},
@{tt}provider_routing@{utt}, @{tt}plugins@{utt} and the other prompt-cache
keys.

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  models:
    gpt-realtime-2.1:
      provider: openai
      model: gpt-realtime-2.1
      api_key: ${OPENAI_API_KEY}
      context_window: 128000
      request_timeout: 240
      capabilities:
        tools: true
        audio_input: false
        image_input: true
        streaming: true
        supported_api_types: [realtime]
        default_api_type: realtime
@{body}
@endnode

@node apikeys "API keys and endpoints"
@{h1}API keys and endpoints

An @{tt}api_key@{utt} in the entry always wins. Without one, the key follows
the @{b}endpoint@{ub} the entry talks to, as on the httpx routes
(@{" API keys and endpoints " link llm_openai_compat/apikeys}): OPENAI_API_KEY
for api.openai.com and for a local host, OPENROUTER_API_KEY for
openrouter.ai, TYPESAFE_API_KEY for api.typesafe.ai, and for any other host
nothing -- building the client fails with a message naming the host. A
missing gateway key therefore never sends the OpenAI key to a third party.

The Realtime socket follows @{tt}base_url@{utt} too:
@{tt}https://host/v1@{utt} becomes @{tt}wss://host/v1/realtime@{utt}.

TLS verification follows the app's setting (@{tt}network.ssl_verify@{utt}),
for the SDK's HTTP client and for the Realtime socket alike.
@endnode

@node timeouts "Timeouts and streaming"
@{h1}Timeouts and streaming

@{h2}Streaming or not

A model streams unless its entry says @{tt}capabilities.streaming:
false@{utt}. Streaming is what lets the web UI show text as it arrives.
Thinking deltas (a @{tt}reasoning_content@{utt} or @{tt}reasoning@{utt} field
some compatible servers send) are streamed as well and kept on the answer;
api.openai.com sends none on Chat Completions.

@{h2}request_timeout

One number, 120 s unless the entry sets it, used three ways:

@{bullet}The SDK's timeout for each phase of an HTTP request (connect,
read, write, pool).
@{bullet}Streaming: the longest gap between two pieces of the stream. A
longer gap is a stalled stream and is retried like a dropped connection.
@{bullet}Realtime: the longest wait for the next event on the socket.

@{tt}llm_system.httpx_timeouts@{utt} and an entry's own
@{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt} are not read by this provider.

@{h2}How a stream ends

@{bullet}The stream's end is the answer, with the finish reason of its last
choice (@{tt}stop@{utt}, @{tt}length@{utt}, @{tt}tool_calls@{utt}, ...).
@{bullet}A stream that stalls or drops is retried from scratch (see
@{" Retries " link retries}); the caller is told to drop what it has shown.
@{bullet}A cancelled run stops before the client reads the next piece; the
cancel reaches the agent as a cancel, never as an answer.
@{bullet}A caller that stops reading closes the request at once: the HTTP
response is closed and the end is reported to the hooks.
@endnode

@node retries "Retries"
@{h1}Retries

The client retries on its own; the SDK's built-in retries are switched off,
so every request that goes out is one the hooks see. Up to five attempts in
all, streaming or not, shared by every kind of retry below. The number and
the waits are fixed in the code; a model entry cannot change them.

@{table}
What happened | What the client does
HTTP 429 | retry; wait: the Retry-After header, else the larger of x-ratelimit-reset-requests and x-ratelimit-reset-tokens (seconds, or units like @{tt}6m0s@{utt}, @{tt}200ms@{utt}) -- never less than the backoff; without any header the backoff plus 0-0.5 s. Then LLMRateLimitError
429 naming a wait longer than 300 s | no retry: LLMRateLimitError at once, with that wait as retry_after, so the agent server blocks the model for it and falls back
429 with "quota" or "exhausted" in the text (insufficient_quota) | no retry: LLMQuotaExhaustedError at once
HTTP 5xx | retry after the backoff, then LLMServerError
connect or read timeout, network error, stalled or dropped stream | retry after the backoff, then LLMConnectionError
any other status (400, 401, 403, 404, ...) | no retry: httpx.HTTPStatusError
@{body}

The backoff doubles from 2 s: 2, 4, 8, 16 s. Every retry is reported to the
post_llm_response hooks as its own row (@{tt}[RETRY n/5] Rate limit
(429)@{utt}, @{tt}Server error (503)@{utt}, @{tt}Network error: ...@{utt};
finish reason @{tt}retry@{utt}) and as a status line in the chat. A
cancelled run interrupts every wait.

A retry starts from scratch: when the caller has already received deltas of
the failed attempt, the stream first sends one
@{tt}{"type": "stream_restart"}@{utt} chunk, and the caller drops what it has
shown of that attempt.

The Realtime path does not retry (see @{" The Realtime API " link realtime}).
The agent server's own fallback chain (@{tt}llm_profile@{utt} with more than
one profile) takes over where these retries end; see
@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}.
@endnode

@node realtime "The Realtime API"
@{h1}The Realtime API

A model whose entry says @{tt}capabilities.default_api_type: realtime@{utt}
is served over the Realtime API instead of Chat Completions -- for
@{tt}chat@{utt}, @{tt}chat_tools@{utt} and streaming alike. The value
@{tt}assistants@{utt} is refused with NotImplementedError; anything else
means Chat Completions.

@{h2}One call, one socket

@{bullet}Each call opens one WebSocket and sends one @{tt}response.create@{utt}
with @{tt}conversation: "none"@{utt}: the whole history travels as
@{tt}input@{utt} items, nothing lingers on the server between calls.
@{bullet}A leading system message becomes @{tt}instructions@{utt}; a later
system or developer message stays a system item at its place.
@{bullet}Output is text only. The client first sends a
@{tt}session.update@{utt} with @{tt}output_modalities: ["text"]@{utt}: set on
the session, not on the response -- on the response, gpt-realtime-2.1
reported @{tt}input_tokens: 0@{utt} (measured 17.09.2026), and the call would
look free.
@{bullet}@{tt}max_tokens@{utt} goes as @{tt}max_output_tokens@{utt}; the
ceiling is the model's. @{tt}temperature@{utt} and @{tt}modalities@{utt} are
not part of the Realtime API and are not sent (logged once per client).
@{bullet}Tools go in the Realtime API's flat form; @{tt}additionalProperties@{utt}
is removed from their parameters.

@{h2}Waiting

Connecting waits up to 10 s for the handshake and 10 s for
@{tt}session.created@{utt}; after that every event waits at most
@{tt}request_timeout@{utt}. A closed or silent socket ends the call with an
error answer. A handshake the server refuses with a status (401, 403,
404: a dead key or model) raises that status like the HTTP path does (see
@{" Errors " link errors}). A cancel ends the call at once, while waiting
for an event too; a request cancelled before it is sent is not sent.

@{h2}The answer

@{bullet}Text streams as @{tt}response.output_text.delta@{utt}; a tool
call's name arrives in @{tt}response.output_item.added@{utt}, its arguments
as deltas. The result -- text, tool calls, usage -- is read from
@{tt}response.done@{utt}.
@{bullet}A response cut at @{tt}max_output_tokens@{utt} or by the content
filter keeps its text, with finish reason @{tt}length@{utt} or
@{tt}content_filter@{utt}. Any other status than @{tt}completed@{utt}, and
an @{tt}error@{utt} event, is an error answer (see
@{" Errors " link errors}).

@{h2}Media

Images go as @{tt}input_image@{utt} -- from user messages and, as on the chat
path, a tool result's attachments as a user message right after its output.
Audio input is never sent, whatever @{tt}audio_input@{utt} says: an audio
part becomes a note that audio input is not supported.

@{h2}Known gap

No retry and no LLMRateLimitError: how the Realtime API reports a rate limit
has not been observed yet. Until then a limit is an ordinary error answer,
and the agent falls back to the next profile.
@endnode

@node usage "Usage, cost and the hooks"
@{h1}Usage, cost and the hooks

Each request is reported to the @{tt}pre_llm_request@{utt} hooks before it
is sent, and to the @{tt}post_llm_response@{utt} hooks when it ends -- exactly
once, however it ends. The message debugger, the OpenTelemetry chat span and
the cost figures are built from these reports
(@{" Hooks " link scarabhive/hooks},
@{" The capture hooks " link message_debugger/hooks},
@{" What gets exported " link otel/spans},
@{" What it records " link context_usage_tracker/hooks}).

@{h2}One report per ending

@{table}
The request ended with | post_llm_response carries
an answer | usage and finish reason
a retry | an error @{tt}[RETRY n/5] ...@{utt}, finish reason retry
a refusal after the retries | the error of the exception raised: @{tt}Rate limit exceeded: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Quota exhausted: ...@{utt}, @{tt}HTTP 500: ...@{utt}, @{tt}HTTP 404: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Network/protocol error: ...@{utt}
a cancel before it was sent, or while it was out | the error @{tt}Request cancelled by user@{utt} (@{tt}cancelled@{utt} when the run's task was cancelled); for a stream the usage too, when its chunk had arrived
a cancel during a retry wait | the error @{tt}Request cancelled during retry wait@{utt}, after the retry's row
a stream the caller stopped reading | the error @{tt}stream abandoned by the caller@{utt}
anything unexpected | the exception's text
@{body}

On the Realtime path the same holds, except that a call whose token is
already cancelled when it starts reports nothing, not even the
pre_llm_request; a cancel later on -- while attachments are encoded or the
socket connects -- is reported like any other. An @{tt}error@{utt} event or a
failed response reports its message.

@{h2}The usage

@{bullet}Chat Completions: @{tt}prompt_tokens@{utt} including cached tokens,
@{tt}prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens@{utt} as the subset,
@{tt}completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens@{utt} as the provider
counts them -- a detail of the completion, never computed from it; a count
above completion_tokens is what the provider sent. Streams ask for usage
with @{tt}stream_options.include_usage@{utt}.
@{bullet}Realtime: mapped to the same shape -- input_tokens to prompt_tokens,
output_tokens to completion_tokens, the cached input tokens and (where the
model reports them) the reasoning tokens as details. Audio tokens are not
reported apart.
@{bullet}Cost: the core prices the tokens from
@{tt}config/llm_pricing.yaml@{utt}.

A cancelled stream is billed for what the model generated, but its usage
comes last, so the report mostly has none.
@endnode

@node errors "Errors"
@{h1}Errors

What reaches the agent server when a request cannot be answered. The server
decides from the type whether to fall back to the next profile of the chain,
and whether to block the model for a while
(@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}).

@{table}
Raised | When
LLMRateLimitError | 429 after the retries, or at once when it names a wait longer than 300 s
LLMQuotaExhaustedError | a 429 with "quota" or "exhausted" in the text, at once
LLMServerError | 5xx after the retries
httpx.HTTPStatusError | any other status, at once; the message is @{tt}HTTP <status>: Error code: <status> - <body>@{utt}. A context that is too long is a 400 like any other. Also a Realtime handshake refused with 4xx (@{tt}HTTP <status>: <body>@{utt}); refused with 429 or 5xx it is LLMRateLimitError or LLMServerError
LLMConnectionError | timeout, stall, dropped stream or network error after the retries
asyncio.CancelledError | the run was cancelled
NotImplementedError | capabilities.default_api_type is assistants
ValueError at build time | no key for the endpoint
RuntimeError at build time | the openai package is not installed
@{body}

@{h2}Errors that come as an answer

An unexpected failure (anything above does not cover) arrives as an answer
with empty content and an @{tt}error@{utt} of type @{tt}openai_api_error@{utt}
on the assistant message; the agent server asks the model once more, then
falls back. On the Realtime path every other failure is such an answer: type
@{tt}upstream_error_<type>@{utt} for an error the API sent,
@{tt}realtime_api_error@{utt} for a socket that closed or went silent.

@{h2}chat()

The plain text call returns the answer's text. An error answer comes back as
the JSON text @{tt}{"_llm_error": {"error": true, "message": ...}}@{utt};
the exceptions above are raised as they are.
@endnode

@node batch "Batch"
@{h1}Batch

A model entry with @{tt}provider: batch@{utt} and @{tt}batch_provider:
openai@{utt} runs its requests through the OpenAI Batch API. The
implementation is not in this plugin: the Batch API is plain HTTP, so it
lives with the httpx clients, and this provider hands the entry to
llm_openai_compat's backend through the registry. What it sends, where, and
how it retries is described there:
@{" Batch and speech " link llm_openai_compat/batch}. Without
llm_openai_compat installed, @{tt}batch_provider: openai@{utt} fails with an
ImportError naming it.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}Messages

@{bullet}Only fields the API knows are sent: internal bookkeeping fields
and a stored @{tt}reasoning_content@{utt} are removed.
@{bullet}Developer notes keep the @{tt}developer@{utt} role unless the
entry's @{tt}capabilities.developer_role@{utt} lowers it. A note that ends
the request goes as a user message wrapped in note tags, whatever the rung
-- a request that ends on something needing no answer gets none.
@{bullet}Audio and video parts of a user message are not sent on Chat
Completions.
@{bullet}Images and other attachments a tool returns follow the tool result
as a user message; a model without @{tt}image_input@{utt} gets a text note
in their place.

@{h2}Tools

Tool schemas go out as the agent built them; a flat schema
(@{tt}name@{utt}, @{tt}description@{utt}, @{tt}parameters@{utt} without a
@{tt}function@{utt} wrapper) is wrapped, a schema without a name is skipped
with a warning. @{tt}tool_choice@{utt} is @{tt}auto@{utt}; a request without
tools carries neither field.

@{h2}Structured output

A response format is sent as @{tt}response_format@{utt} (JSON schema or JSON
mode) when the entry declares @{tt}capabilities.structured_output@{utt}; a
call asking for a format the entry does not declare is refused rather than
answered with free text. The Realtime API has no such field.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing to enable: the core finds LLM provider plugins by their
@{tt}plugin.toml@{utt} (@{tt}type = ["llm-provider"]@{utt}) and loads
@{tt}provider.py@{utt} when a model entry names one of its providers. There
is no entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} and no allowlist line.

@{table}
Manifest key | Names
provides | openai
provides_batch | openai (delegated, see @{" Batch " link batch})
default_base_url | openai: https://api.openai.com/v1
@{body}

@{h2}Dependencies

@{tt}openai>=1.37.0@{utt}, imported when a client is built. The Realtime
path needs @{tt}websockets@{utt}, imported when a call connects.

@{h2}Where things live

Nothing on disk. Changes to @{tt}llm.yaml@{utt} reach an agent when it
builds its client, i.e. after a restart.
@endnode
